**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — ready to use. Key details:

- **`getDerivedStateFromError`** captures the error into state to trigger the fallback render.
- **`componentDidCatch`** logs `message`, `stack`, and `componentStack` to the console.
- **`retry`** resets state to `null`, which re-renders children from scratch.
- **`fallback` prop** lets callers supply a custom UI; otherwise `DefaultFallback` is shown.

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(err, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>{err.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

> **Note:** Error boundaries only catch errors during rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors of child components — not inside event handlers or async code. For those, use `try/catch` or `window.onerror`.
